<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Can menopause cause sudden hearing loss?</h3>
<p>Sudden hearing loss isn’t typical of menopause and should be treated as urgent. If you notice a rapid drop in hearing over hours to a day—especially in one ear—seek prompt evaluation from an ENT or urgent care. Early treatment can make a difference.</p>

<h3>Will hormone therapy improve my tinnitus or hearing?</h3>
<p>Hormone therapy isn’t a proven treatment for tinnitus or hearing loss. Some observational studies suggest certain hormone regimens may be associated with changes in hearing risk, while others show no clear effect. If you use hormone therapy for vasomotor or other menopausal symptoms, simply add routine hearing monitoring and focus on proven tinnitus and hearing supports.</p>

<h3>Are hearing aids worth it for mild hearing loss?</h3>
<p>Often, yes—especially if you struggle in noise, feel fatigued after conversations, or have bothersome tinnitus. Modern hearing aids reduce listening effort, improve clarity in challenging settings, and can provide built-in sound therapy. An audiologist can help you decide based on your test and goals.</p>

<h3>Is dizziness during perimenopause normal?</h3>
<p>Hormonal fluctuations can interact with balance systems and migraine biology, so occasional dizziness can show up. However, recurrent, severe, or positional vertigo should be evaluated, because conditions like benign positional vertigo are common and treatable.</p>


